<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<ValidationRule xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT, NOT IN <description>, AND IT MUST STAY THAT WAY.
        ValidationRule.Description is capped at 255 characters. The rationale below is
        operationally important, so it was moved here rather than trimmed; a comment is
        repo-only and is never sent to the Metadata API. Do not paste this back into
        <description>. NOTE ALSO: the comment must sit INSIDE the root element - a comment
        placed ABOVE <ValidationRule> breaks `sf` deploy at conversion with a misleading
        "unable to find matching parent xml file" error.

        =====================================================================================
        THIS FILE REPLACES Wire_Complete_Before_Completed. IT IS NOT A RENAME.
        =====================================================================================
        A validation rule API-name change is a DELETE plus a CREATE - the Metadata API has no
        rename operation for one. The old rule is retired through
        manifest/disposition-redesign-destructive/destructiveChangesPost.xml and MUST be deleted
        in the same deploy wave that lands this file, or the org briefly carries both: the old
        one gates a stage value that still exists (Completed) and the new one gates its
        replacement (Sale Closes). With zero rows on Completed that overlap is harmless, but
        leaving the old rule behind permanently would block the manual deletion of the Completed
        picklist value, which is the last step of this programme.

        THE WIRE GATE. A disposition cannot reach Sale Closes while the wire-fraud check is
        incomplete. Wire_Verification_Completed__c is DERIVED - the Wire_Verification_Rollup flow
        sets it from the MOST RECENT Wire__c on the disposition (the same row
        WireSelector.selectMostRecentByDispositionId returns), true only when all six
        confirmation fields are populated.

        WHY OR(ISNEW(), ISCHANGED(...)): ISCHANGED alone gates the transition but leaves a
        create-at-Sale-Closes hole. ISNEW closes it. Together they still do NOT trap rows already
        sitting at Sale Closes, whose every subsequent edit would otherwise be blocked. Same
        shape as the deployed Property_Registry__c.Winning_Lead_Required.

        =====================================================================================
        🔴 THE Q4 ARGUMENT THIS RULE'S PREDECESSOR CARRIED IS RETRACTED, AND Closing_Approval
        NOW DOES THE THING Q4 FORBADE. QUOTED, NOT DELETED, BECAUSE IT IS EXACTLY WHAT A FUTURE
        READER WOULD RESTORE FROM.
        =====================================================================================

            RETRACTED: "WHY THIS SHAPE, NOT AN APPROVAL entryCriteria (design Q4): Putting the
            flag in Closing_Approval's entryCriteria would surface an unmet gate as the
            platform's 'no applicable approval process was found' - a misleading error the user
            cannot act on. Instead the flag is an approvalPageFields entry on Closing_Approval so
            the approver SEES the wire state when deciding, and the hard stop lives here where
            its message renders natively in the Path UI."

        WHY IT DIED - THE PREMISE, NOT THE PRINCIPLE. Q4 was right that an unmet gate must not
        surface as the platform's unhelpful message. Its premise was that entry criteria are the
        ONLY place a user would meet that gate. Submission no longer runs through the platform's
        generic Submit button: it runs through the Submit_Closing_Approval quick action, backed
        by DispositionApprovalService.submitForApproval, which PRE-CHECKS this same flag and
        raises an AUTHORED message naming the six wire confirmation fields. The user never
        reaches the platform's message. 🔴 THAT PRE-CHECK IS THE LOAD-BEARING HALF: if it is ever
        removed, Closing_Approval's Wire_Verification_Completed__c entry criterion must be
        removed with it, or Q4's failure mode returns.
        And under the new flow the criterion is not optional anyway - without it the AdminOnly
        approval lock blocks Wire_Verification_Rollup for the whole pending window, and the
        approval would then fire a stage advance this rule refuses. Closing_Approval's own XML
        comment carries that deadlock analysis in full.

        🔴 THIS RULE IS NOT REPLACED BY THAT ENTRY CRITERION. BOTH GATES STAY AND THEY GUARD
        DIFFERENT THINGS. The entry criterion decides who may ENTER the approval. This rule
        blocks any write of 'Sale Closes' from any source - including a hand edit that never went
        near an approval, and including the machine writes below. Do not delete either on the
        grounds that the other exists.

        =====================================================================================
        FIRING SURFACE - THIS RULE NOW FIRES AGAINST A SECOND MACHINE WRITER, BY DESIGN.
        =====================================================================================
        Its predecessor's comment recorded ContractExecutionService as the first machine writer
        of Disposition_Stage__c and noted that it "moves records INTO Closing and never out of
        it ... so it cannot trip this gate". That is STILL TRUE of that service.
        🔴 WHAT IS NEW: DispositionApprovalAdvanceQueueable (design D-1) writes 'Sale Closes'
        itself, on final approval of Closing_Approval. So unlike its predecessor, THIS RULE CAN
        FIRE AGAINST A WRITE NO HUMAN MADE, and that is intended. The Queueable's DML is
        Database.update(..., false, AccessLevel.SYSTEM_MODE): SYSTEM_MODE bypasses CRUD and FLS,
        NOT validation rules.
        ⚠ IN PRACTICE IT SHOULD NEVER TRIP ON THAT PATH, because Closing_Approval cannot be
        entered unless the flag is already true. The residual is narrow and fail-closed: if an
        admin unticks a verbal verification between submission and the Queueable running, this
        rule refuses the advance and Approval_Advance_Pending__c is LEFT TRUE - visible by SOQL
        on that flag, recoverable, and far better than a silent skip.
        ⚠ ALSO STILL TRUE, AND WORTH KEEPING TRUE: no FLOW and no APPROVAL FIELD UPDATE writes
        Disposition_Stage__c. The D-1 field update writes a semaphore flag precisely so that it
        does not have to, because an approval field update WOULD bypass this rule.

        DEPLOY ORDER - LOAD-BEARING. The Wire_Verification_Rollup flow MUST already be deployed.
        Deploying this rule against no rollup leaves Wire_Verification_Completed__c false
        everywhere with nothing able to set it, so NO disposition could reach Sale Closes.

        BLAST RADIUS OF ISNEW() - for the developer and testing agents. Any test or seed script
        that INSERTS a Disposition__c directly at Sale Closes will fail. Sweep
        TestDataFactory.createDispositions, DispositionSelectorTest, DispositionControllerTest,
        BovControllerTest, WireServiceTest, WireControllerTest and the disposition seed scripts -
        and note that any of those that were updated to say 'Completed' must now say
        'Sale Closes', which is the SAME sweep the old rule's own blast-radius note demanded.
    -->
    <fullName>Wire_Complete_Before_Sale_Closes</fullName>
    <active>true</active>
    <description>Blocks the Closing to Sale Closes transition (and create-at-Sale-Closes) while Wire_Verification_Completed__c is false. That flag is derived by the Wire_Verification_Rollup flow. It also gates the D-1 approval auto-advance.</description>
    <errorConditionFormula>AND(
    ISPICKVAL(Disposition_Stage__c, &apos;Sale Closes&apos;),
    OR(ISNEW(), ISCHANGED(Disposition_Stage__c)),
    NOT(Wire_Verification_Completed__c)
)</errorConditionFormula>
    <errorDisplayField>Disposition_Stage__c</errorDisplayField>
    <errorMessage>The wire verification is not complete. All six wire confirmation fields must be confirmed on the Wire record before this disposition can reach Sale Closes.</errorMessage>
</ValidationRule>
